      "raw_mention": "S&P",
      "text": "I found the most fascinating divergence in global macro.\n\nThe most recent South Korean consumer sentiment reading sits above 100, meaning optimists outnumber pessimists, and has held near multi-year highs. \n\nMeanwhile U.S. consumer sentiment is at its lowest on record. \n\nSo why does one country feel rich and the other feel broke? Both countries saw their stock markets rip to record highs this year. \n\nThe KOSPI is up 57% compared to the S&P at just 9.5% YTD, that could explain it. But it's not necessarily the returns that create the divergence.\n\nIt's HOW citizens in each country own stocks.\n\nKorea has 105 million active brokerage accounts in a country of 51 million people. Retail is roughly half of all trading volume. Margin debt just hit an all time high of 38.5 trillion won. Samsung and SK Hynix alone account for 51% of daily market turnover, and once you add the single stock leverage and inverse ETFs, it clears 80%.\n\nKoreans don't have \"exposure\" to the market. They ARE the market. Direct, concentrated, levered. The KOSPI doubled in 9 months and every kitchen table in Seoul FELT it. That's your consumer sentiment print.\n\nAmerica is the opposite. 58% of families own stock but only 21% picked individual stocks, and that ownership number has been flat near 20% for three decades. The percentage of Americans trying to make multi-bagger returns is a tiny fraction of Koreans. \n\nBecause of this, Americans have exposure to the markets but don't really feel it. They glance at a quarterly 401k statement moving up with the index and go back to paying for gas. They're not building an active trading strategy around thematics or speculating on AI bottlenecks.\n\nFable 5 tells me only about half of Americans might recognize Nvidia by name, even less could tell you what it does. Meanwhile, 99% of Korean people know their largest companies like Samsung and SK Hynix intimatley.\n\nOf course, it's likely that the next consumer sentiment reading in Korea will be markedly lower after the country's stock market has fallen 20% in just the last month. Leverage, speculation, and active trading around a couple of companies is a two way street.\n\nBut certainly a fascinating difference between two economies at the center of the AI revolution.",
